ng F2, so maybe not F1.\n\nBetween T4 and F1, F1 is an honor tile, which is more likely to be part of a pair or Pung. So discarding T4 might be better.\n\nAlternatively, W3 is a suited tile with 4 left. Discarding W3 is safe since I have three in hand, and it's a Pung. But since I need to keep the Pung for scoring, maybe it's better to keep it. So discard T4.\n\nWait, but T4 is part of a potential Chi if I can get T5 and T6. But since I don't have those, it's not useful. So discarding T4 is safe.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to discard T4. It's a middle tile with three left in the wall, less likely to be needed by others, and not part of any current meld. This keeps my hand flexible for forming other melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discard T4 as it is a middle tile with three copies remaining in the wall, less likely to be needed for immediate wins, and not part of any current meld. This maintains flexibility for forming additional melds or pairs.]\nReason: [T4 is a middle tile with three remaining copies, reducing risk of giving others a winning tile. Keeping W3 (Pung) and other key tiles preserves scoring potential and flexibility.]\nAnswer: Play T4"}
