ible combinations.\n\nIf I can form a Chi of W2-W3-W4 (needs one W2, one W3, one W4). I have one W2, two W3s, one W4. So if I discard one W3, then I have one W3 left, but that's not enough for the Chi. Wait, to form a Chi, you need three tiles in sequence. So if I have W2, W3, W4, that's a Chi. I have one W2, two W3s, one W4. So if I discard one W3, I have one W3 left, but I can't form the Chi. But if I keep both W3s, then I can form the Chi if I have another W2 or W4. But I don't. So maybe not helpful. Alternatively, maybe I can form a Pung of W3 if I get another. But I have two, so need one more.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a pair of W9s. If I have two W9s, that's a pair. Then the remaining tiles would need three melds. Let's see:\n\n- Pair W9s: 2 tiles.\n- Then need three melds from the remaining 9 tiles: W2, W3, W3, W4, T5, T7, B5, F1, J1.\n\nPossible melds:\n\n- Chi T1-T2-T3 (already there).\n- Maybe another Chi in W2-W3-W4. If I have W2, W3, W4. But I have one W2, two W3s, one W4. So if I can get another W3, but I have two. Wait, no. If I have W2, W3, W4, that's three tiles. So if I can form that Chi, then that's a meld. But I need to have those three. Let's see: I have one W2, two W3s, one W4. So if I can use one W3, then I have W2, W3, W4. That's a Chi. Then the remaining W3 can be part of another meld or pair. But after forming the Chi, I have one W3 left. Then maybe another meld? Let's see.\n\nAfter forming Chi W2-W3-W4, remaining tiles: W3, T5, T7, B5, F1, J1. Need two more melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\n- Maybe a pair of W3? But only one W3 left. Not enough.\n- T5-T6-T7 Chi? If I can get T6. But I don't have it. So not possible.\n- B5-B6-B7 Chi? Need B6 and B7. Not in hand.\n- So maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W3, maybe I can form a different combination. Let's think again.\n\nIf I discard W3, my hand becomes:\n\nW2, W3, W4, W9, W9, T5, T7, B5, F1, J1. (Wait, original hand was W2:1, W3:2, W4:1, W9:2, etc. If I discard one W3, t